What the error means (short)
- org.hibernate.LazyInitializationException: failed to lazily initialize a collection ... could not initialize proxy - no Session
- This means Hibernate tried to load a lazily‑fetched association (a collection or proxy) but there was no open Hibernate Session / transactional context available at the moment of access, so it cannot perform the SQL needed to initialize the data.

Why this happens in your project (precise)
- Your `DailyLog` entity has these associations:
  - `@OneToMany(cascade = CascadeType.ALL) private List<Meal> meals;` (same for liftingExercises and cardioExercises)
  - `@OneToMany` defaults to LAZY fetch, so these collections are not loaded from the DB when the `DailyLog` entity is first retrieved.
- The mapper `DailyLogMapper.mapToDailyLogDto(...)` reads `dailyLog.getMeals()` (and the other collections):
  - file: `src/main/java/.../mapper/DailyLogMapper.java`
  - method: `mapToDailyLogDto(final DailyLog dailyLog)` reads `dailyLog.getMeals()` etc.
- `DailyLogServiceImpl.getAllLogsForUser(...)` calls the repository and then maps the returned entities to DTOs:
  - file: `src/main/java/.../service/implaily/DLogServiceImpl.java`
  - `final List<DailyLog> dailyLogs = dailyLogRepository.findByUserUsername(username);`
  - then `dailyLogs.stream().map(dailyLogMapper::mapToDailyLogDto)...`
- The repository method `findByUserUsername(...)` is a Spring Data JPA method that runs in its own transactional boundary. Because your service methods are not annotated with `@Transactional`, that repository transaction begins and ends inside the repository call; when control returns to the service, the session is closed. Mapping (which tries to access the lazy collections) happens after the repository call, so the collections cannot be initialized and Hibernate throws the exception.

Fix options (ranked by use recommended)

1) Add a transactional boundary to the service method(s) so the session is open while mapping (simple, low-risk)
- Add the Spring transaction annotation so the database session stays open while mapping:

