Notes:
- `@Transactional(readOnly = true)` at class level keeps read calls transactional and open; mutating methods (save/delete) should be either in a different service or annotated `@Transactional` (without readOnly) so they can write.
- This is the easiest change and will eliminate the LazyInitializationException because the session is open while your mapper accesses the collections.

2) Fetch associations eagerly in the repository (explicit fetch join or @EntityGraph)
- If you always need collections when loading logs, load them with the query to avoid lazy loading.

Example using `@EntityGraph` in `DailyLogRepository`:

```java
import org.springframework.data.jpa.repository.EntityGraph;

@EntityGraph(attributePaths = {"meals", "liftingExercises", "cardioExercises"})
List<DailyLog> findByUserUsername(String username);
```

Or using JPQL fetch join:

```java
@Query("SELECT DISTINCT d FROM DailyLog d LEFT JOIN FETCH d.meals LEFT JOIN FETCH d.liftingExercises LEFT JOIN FETCH d.cardioExercises WHERE d.user.username = :username")
List<DailyLog> findByUserUsernameWithCollections(@Param("username") String username);
```

- Use these when you want to control exactly what associations are fetched and to avoid N+1 problems.

3) Map to DTOs in the repository (DTO projection)
- Return DTOs directly from a repository query (projection) so mapping happens within the query and no lazy init is needed. This is the most efficient for large data sets but requires writing projection interfaces or custom queries.

4) Change fetch type to EAGER (not recommended generally)
- You could set `@OneToMany(fetch = FetchType.EAGER)` on the collections, but this often causes performance issues (unnecessary data loading, larger queries and potential Cartesian product problems). Prefer targeted fetching (`@EntityGraph`/fetch join) or transactional mapping.

